        body: "**Spot Instances** use spare EC2 capacity at up to 90% off On-Demand. The catch: AWS can reclaim them with a **2-minute interruption notice** (visible in instance metadata and as an EventBridge event). AWS may also send an earlier **rebalance recommendation** when interruption risk rises.\n\nBest practices that appear in answers:\n\n- Use an Auto Scaling group or EC2 Fleet with a **mixed instances policy**: many instance types and all AZs, so one pool running out does not stop you.\n- Use the **capacity-optimized** or **price-capacity-optimized** allocation strategy (AWS's recommended default) to launch from the deepest capacity pools, minimizing interruptions.\n- Keep a base of On-Demand (e.g. 2 instances) for the minimum service level and fill the rest with Spot.\n- Design workloads to be stateless or checkpointing (batch jobs, CI, rendering, big-data task nodes). Never put a single-instance production database on Spot.",
